# rule.require.catalog-is-an-index

a catalog is an index: one row per member, no narrative about any one. it takes the itemization form (`catalog.of=$concept._.md` plus `$dimension=$position.md` per member).

## .three grains

| artifact | grain | holds |
|---|---|---|
| catalog | the set | one row per member: pattern, rule, detect question |
| rule | the class | mechanism, detect question, ONE worked case, fix, enforcement |
| `.example=$id.md` | one occurrence | date, id, sweep table, results, residual |

## .the test, before a paragraph lands

is this about the class (→ rule), the set (→ catalog), or one occurrence (→ `example=$id.md`)? prose about the set (classification, through-line, named gap) is what a catalog is for. prose about one member is the violation.

repair is ejection, never a trim: move each occurrence record to its own `example=$id.md`, leave the row.

blocker: `###` narrative about one member added to a catalog · occurrence record with no `example=$id.md` home · catalog whose narrative exceeds its index.
false positive: prose about the set.

⇒ `rule.require.rules-are-clusters` · `rule.require.inventory-for-enumerable-concepts`
